  <description promeva:title="Description (English)" promeva:type="104" type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Global Programme Overview  

CASCADE (CAtalyzing Strengthened policy aCtion for heAlthy Diets and resiliencE) is a multiyear programme (2022&#x2013;2026) funded by the Netherlands Ministry of Foreign Affairs that aims to improve food security, reduce malnutrition, and strengthen resilience among women of reproductive age and children in six African countries (Benin, Ethiopia, Kenya, Mozambique, Nigeria, Uganda). The programme is implemented as part of a global consortium, led by CARE Nederland in partnership with the Global Alliance for Improved Nutrition (GAIN), and brings together a diverse group of international and national partners with complementary technical expertise, policy experience, and country presence. Working through this consortium model, CASCADE enables cross-country learning, harmonized approaches to nutrition governance, and the application of global evidence to country-specific contexts. The project focuses on strengthening the implementation of existing nutrition policies, enhancing multi&#x2011;sectoral coordination, engaging the private sector, and addressing structural and gender-related barriers to healthy diets through a food systems approach, while leveraging consortium-wide knowledge sharing and collaboration to support sustainable, government-led nutrition outcomes. 

The purpose of CASCADE is to contribute to improved food security and reduced malnutrition among women of reproductive age and children. This is achieved through two core objectives: 

Increased access to and consumption of healthy diets. 

Increased resilience of household nutritional status to price and climate change related shocks, particularly for women and children. 

Activities are organized around five interlinked pathways: 

Domain 1: Nutrition policy engagement and implementation: strengthening national and district level implementation of nutrition policies, improving functionality of District Nutrition Coordination Committees, and supporting integration of nutrition priorities into development planning, financing, and budgeting. 

Domain 2: Private sector engagement: strengthening private service providers&#x2019; roles in producing, supplying, and marketing nutritious foods, including expansion of the SUN Business Network. 

Domain 3: Civil society engagement and accountability: supporting civil society actors to engage in nutrition policy processes, advocacy, and accountability mechanisms at local and national levels. 

Domain 4: Women&#x2019;s and household engagement: working with women&#x2019;s groups and households to increase agency, access to resources, and adoption of improved nutrition and climate smart agriculture practices, while addressing harmful gender norms. 

Domain 5: Data, learning, and coordination: collecting, systematizing, and disseminating data and evidence to inform policy actions, strengthen coordination, and support adaptive management. 

Concrete actions include establishment of agricultural demonstration and learning sites, development of nutrition planning and budgeting tools, district level ordinance development, and capacity strengthening across public, private, and community stakeholders. 

Rationale for the Intervention in Uganda 

The CASCADE programme is highly relevant to the Uganda context, as the country continues to face high levels of malnutrition and poor dietary outcomes, particularly among women and children. Approximately 29% of children under five are stunted, 53% are anaemic, and 32% of women aged 15&#x2013;49 are anaemic. Access to and consumption of healthy, affordable diets remains limited, especially for vulnerable populations. These challenges are compounded by weak implementation of existing nutrition policies, limited coordination across sectors, insufficient private sector engagement in nutritious food provision, harmful gender norms affecting food and nutrition practices, and households&#x2019; vulnerability to price and climate&#x2011;related shocks. The intervention addresses these systemic gaps by strengthening policy implementation, coordination, financing, and accountability, while promoting resilience and gender equality within food systems. 

Programme goals and objectives in Uganda include contributing to improved food security and reducing malnutrition among women of reproductive age and children, in line with national priorities set out in the Uganda Nutrition Action Plan II 2020&#x2013;2025 (UNAP II). To support UNAP II implementation, CASCADE pursues two core objectives in the Uganda context: increasing access to and consumption of healthy diets and strengthening the resilience of household nutritional status to price and climate&#x2011;related shocks, particularly for women and children. The programme supports the translation of UNAP II&#x2019;s multisectoral framework into actionable, coordinated interventions at national and district levels by strengthening nutrition governance, enhancing functionality of coordination mechanisms, and aligning planning, budgeting, and implementation process</narrative>
  </description>
